  "review": {
    "review": "The complete public death receipt identifies a prison guard as the killer, although the compact packet omitted that message. The initiating retreat defect appears earlier: decision 3713 selected closed doorway 840 while the hero wore publicly identified bulk armor and the guard could see that doorway. Its accepted transition advanced about 2.27 turns, recorded a chain pull, and reduced HP from 17 to 2. Decision 3717 then selected another doorway transit and died. The cover selector checked destination concealment and target melee reach but omitted the known doorway slowdown. A focused correction rejects exposed doorway cover routes against pull-capable enemies while wearing known bulk armor, for both new and committed routes. A public-terrain fixture reproduces the unsafe admission on original source and selects the already-admitted ordinary-ground alternative after correction. The final regression has four expected baseline assertion failures and passes all five tests after correction; the full suite passes 2,159 tests with four skips. The fixture's optional plan is synthetic, not recovered original memory. This does not establish survival, future enemy movement, or loot completion. No recorded-code improvements are attributed because the supplied source comparison is empty.",
    "learnings": "Treat known bulk armor as a doorway-specific retreat constraint. A hidden destination does not make exposed transit safe against a guard's pull. Recheck committed routes without assuming chains are spent, and retain all ordinary hazard and mandatory-pickup checks."
  },
